<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Titterstone Clee Hill</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Titterstone Clee</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Titterstone Clee Hill is a hill in south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shropshire">Shropshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, rising to 533 metres. Its summit is enclosed by a large prehistoric hillfort, one of the many fortified hilltops in the land of the Cornovii. In modern times the hill was heavily quarried for dolerite, known locally as dhustone, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> was mined on its slopes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/titterstone-clee-hill.jpg" alt="Titterstone Clee Hill" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Richard Webb · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Titterstone_Clee_Hill.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Titterstone Clee is the third-highest hill in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shropshire">Shropshire</a>, after Brown Clee Hill and the Stiperstones, and belongs to the Clee Hills within the Shropshire Hills National Landscape. The nearest town is Ludlow. The hill stands above the country around it because a thick sill of dolerite and a band of hornfels cap the softer Devonian Old Red Sandstone, Carboniferous limestones and Coal Measures beneath. The dark dolerite, an olivine basalt, takes its local name from the Welsh du, black. Near the summit lies the Giant's Chair, a pile of boulders formed in cold phases of the Devensian glaciation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Near the summit are the remains of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> cairn about 4,000 years old, which suggests a ceremonial use of the hilltop. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> hillfort, though partly damaged by quarrying, has survived better than the fort on Brown Clee, and its walls are unusually built of stone blocks rather than earth banks. Clee Hill is one of the few hills marked on the 13th-century Hereford Mappa Mundi. Ironstone was mined in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> later, often from small bell pits.</p><p>Dhustone was cut into setts for roads and docks, among them the new docks at Newport in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-wales">South Wales</a>, and later crushed for concrete and road surfaces. Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the quarries employed more than 2,000 people, many coming from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bridgnorth">Bridgnorth</a> and Ludlow, and the villages of Bedlam and Dhustone were built to house them. A narrow-gauge incline, a standard-gauge incline used until the early 1960s and an aerial ropeway from the Magpie Quarry to the railway at Detton Ford carried the stone away. Some disused quarry buildings include some of the earliest known uses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reinforced-concrete">reinforced concrete</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radar">radar</a> station, RAF Clee Hill, opened in September 1941 and closed in September 1957.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radar">Radar</a> domes on the summit, one nicknamed the golf ball, serve the national air traffic network and a Met Office weather radar. Quarrying for road stone resumed on Clee Hill in the late 1980s. The upper hill is common land grazed by sheep and open to walkers, and the Shropshire Way crosses it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Titterstone Clee Hill” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their landscape was dominated by hillforts such as the Wrekin, <b>Titterstone Clee</b> and the Breiddin, many of which had been occupied for centuries before the Roman period.</blockquote><a href="/state/cornovii">Cornovii</a></li></ul></section>
